  "result": "I found and fixed the cause of the 500s. `npm test` now passes (7/7), and the 3 new tests fail when the fix is removed.\n\n**Root cause:** Every one of the 37 checkout 500s in `logs/app.log` is the same `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`. The debug line logged just before each error shows the price had a thousands separator. Only three prices appear: `\"1,299.00\"`, `\"2,450.50\"` and `\"1,005.99\"`. In `src/money.js`, `parseAmount` only accepted plain digits like `\\d+(.\\d{1,2})?`. When the regex found no match it returned `null`, and the next line read `m[1]` from it, which crashed. The errors were intermittent because only orders with an item priced at 1,000 or more were affected.\n\n**Fix (`src/money.js`):**\n- `parseAmount` now accepts properly grouped commas (`1,299.00` and `1,234,567.5`) and removes them before converting to cents.\n- If the input still doesn't match, it throws `Invalid amount: <input>`, which is what the function's comment already said it does. Malformed input like `1,29.00` or `12.345` gets that clear error instead of a null crash.\n\n**Regression tests (`test/checkout.test.js`):**\n- Comma-grouped prices parse to the right number of cents.\n- Malformed amounts are rejected with the `Invalid amount` message.\n- An end-to-end `POST /checkout` through `handle()`, using real prices from the log, returns 200 with the correct total.\n\nOne thing I didn't change: an amount that is genuinely invalid still returns a 500, because the server treats any error as internal. Returning a 400 for bad input would be a separate change in `src/server.js`.\n\nAn untracked `eval/` directory also appears in `git status`; I didn't touch it.",
